<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Selangor Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1766 CE – present · capital Kuala Selangor (later Klang)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bugis-founded Malay sultanate whose tin fields gave rise to Kuala Lumpur, now Malaysia's most populous and prosperous state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> warriors who dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a> in the 18th century settled on the Selangor coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salehuddin-of-selangor">Raja Lumu</a>, son of the Bugis chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daeng-chelak">Daeng Chelak</a>, was recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Salehuddin Shah around 1766, with the sanction of the ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak</a>, and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-selangor">Kuala Selangor</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Selangor was divided among river chiefs who taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> exported from the interior. Chinese miners poured in during the 19th century, and the rivalry of chiefs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> erupted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klang-war">Klang War</a> of 1867 to 1874, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yap-ah-loy">Yap Ah Loy</a> built up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-samad-of-selangor">Sultan Abdul Samad</a> accepted a British resident in 1874.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Selangor joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a> in 1895, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> as federal capital. It became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a> and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, ceding Kuala Lumpur in 1974 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/putrajaya">Putrajaya</a> in 2001 as federal territories. The sultanate continues under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharafuddin-of-selangor">Sharafuddin Idris Shah</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/selangor-sultanate.jpg" alt="Selangor Sultanate" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Lambert &amp;amp; Co., G.R. / Singapore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KITLV_-_105898_-_Lambert_%26_Co.,_G.R._-_Singapore_-_River_in_Kuala_Lumpur_-_circa_1900.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1766 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuala Selangor (later Klang)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salehuddin Shah, Abdul Samad, Sharafuddin Idris Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Selangor, Selangor</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=selangor-sultanate" aria-label="Selangor Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7490 2053 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Selangor Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7810 2309L4997 651M7810 2309L7823 2316M7810 2309L7823 2316M7810 2309L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7810 2309h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7810 2309h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuala Selangor. <a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=selangor-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

